# Environment Setup and Dependency Management (Go)

## Purpose
This rule ensures proper Go module setup, dependency management, and linting toolchain configuration for all Go/Gin projects.

## Mandatory Environment Setup

### 1. Go Toolchain
**ALWAYS** verify the Go toolchain version before starting:

```bash
go version   # Expect go1.23.x or newer
```

Pin the module's minimum Go version in `go.mod`:

```go
module github.com/example/blog-api

go 1.23
```

### 2. Module Initialization

**ALWAYS** initialize the module once, at project creation, using the real (or placeholder) import path:

```bash
go mod init github.com/example/blog-api
```

Never run `go mod init` again after the module exists — subsequent commands are `go get` and `go mod tidy`.

### 3. Dependency Management Rules

**ALWAYS** follow this workflow for dependencies:

1. **Add a pinned dependency**:
   ```bash
   go get github.com/gin-gonic/gin@v1.10.0
   ```

2. **Tidy the module graph immediately after**:
   ```bash
   go mod tidy
   ```

3. **Never hand-edit `go.sum`**:
   ```bash
   # ❌ DON'T DO THIS — never edit go.sum by hand
   # ✅ DO THIS — let the toolchain regenerate it
   go mod tidy
   ```

4. **Never use unpinned `@latest` in committed code** — always pin an explicit version tag so builds are reproducible:
   ```bash
   # ❌ Avoid in course/production code
   go get github.com/gin-gonic/gin@latest

   # ✅ Pin explicitly
   go get github.com/gin-gonic/gin@v1.10.0
   ```

### 4. Project Initialization Checklist

When starting any new project, **ALWAYS**:

1. Initialize the module:
   ```bash
   go mod init github.com/example/project-name
   ```

2. Add core dependencies:
   ```bash
   go get github.com/gin-gonic/gin@v1.10.0
   go get gorm.io/gorm@v1.25.10
   go get gorm.io/driver/postgres@v1.5.9
   go get github.com/stretchr/testify@v1.9.0
   go get github.com/spf13/viper@v1.19.0
   ```

3. Tidy the module:
   ```bash
   go mod tidy
   ```

4. Install `golangci-lint` (once per machine, not per project):
   ```bash
   go install github.com/golangci-lint/golangci-lint/cmd/golangci-lint@latest
   # or on macOS:
   brew install golangci-lint
   ```

5. Add a `.golangci.yml` at the project root (see below).

### 5. Adding New Dependencies

**ALWAYS** follow this exact sequence:

1. **Get the pinned version**:
   ```bash
   go get github.com/some/package@v1.2.3
   ```

2. **Tidy immediately**:
   ```bash
   go mod tidy
   ```

3. **Verify it resolved correctly**:
   ```bash
   go list -m all | grep some/package
   ```

### 6. `.gitignore` Requirements

**ALWAYS** ensure `.gitignore` includes:

```gitignore
# Binaries
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
main
server
/bin/
/dist/

# Test artifacts
*.test
*.out
coverage.out
coverage.html

# Go workspace file (unless intentionally committed)
go.work
go.work.sum

# Vendoring (unless the course intentionally vendors)
vendor/

# Environment
.env
.env.local

# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
```

### 7. `.golangci.yml` Baseline

Every project gets a linter config at the root:

```yaml
run:
  timeout: 5m

linters:
  enable:
    - errcheck      # unchecked errors
    - govet         # suspicious constructs
    - staticcheck   # advanced static analysis
    - gosimple      # simplification suggestions
    - ineffassign   # ineffectual assignments
    - unused        # unused code
    - gofmt
    - goimports
    - bodyclose     # HTTP response bodies must be closed
    - sqlclosecheck # sql.Rows/Stmt must be closed
    - noctx         # HTTP requests without context.Context

linters-settings:
  govet:
    enable-all: true

issues:
  exclude-dirs:
    - vendor
```

Run it before every phase transition:

```bash
golangci-lint run
```

### 8. Docker Integration

When using Docker, leverage layer caching by copying module files before source:

```dockerfile
FROM golang:1.23-alpine AS builder
WORKDIR /app

# Cache dependency downloads in their own layer
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download

# Only now copy source — changes here don't bust the dependency cache
COPY . .
RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -o main ./cmd/server
```

See `rules/500-docker-go.mdc` and `vibe/vibe_docker.md` for the full multi-stage pattern (already
complete in this stack — do not duplicate, just reference).

### 9. CI/CD Integration

In CI/CD pipelines (GitHub Actions example):

```yaml
name: CI

on: [push, pull_request]

jobs:
  build-test-lint:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-go@v5
        with:
          go-version: '1.23'
          cache: true

      - name: Download dependencies
        run: go mod download

      - name: Build
        run: go build ./...

      - name: Vet
        run: go vet ./...

      - name: Lint
        uses: golangci/golangci-lint-action@v6
        with:
          version: latest

      - name: Test
        run: go test ./... -race -cover
```

### 10. GOFLAGS / GOPROXY Notes

- `GOPROXY` defaults to `https://proxy.golang.org,direct` — fine for most environments; only override
  behind a corporate proxy or for private modules (`GOPRIVATE=github.com/your-org/*`).
- `GOFLAGS=-mod=mod` can be set in CI if you want `go build`/`go test` to auto-update `go.mod` on
  missing requires, but prefer explicit `go mod tidy` in course material — it is more teachable.
- `CGO_ENABLED=0` is required for static binaries used in `scratch`/`alpine` Docker images (see
  `rules/500-docker-go.mdc`).

### 11. Common Commands Reference

```bash
# Verify toolchain
go version

# Add and pin a dependency
go get github.com/some/package@v1.2.3

# Tidy the module graph
go mod tidy

# Download without adding (CI cache warm)
go mod download

# Build everything
go build ./...

# Run the server directly
go run ./cmd/server

# Run all tests, verbose, race detector, coverage
go test ./... -v -race -cover

# Format check (should print nothing)
gofmt -l .

# Vet
go vet ./...

# Lint
golangci-lint run
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

**NEVER**:
- Run `go mod init` more than once per module
- Hand-edit `go.sum`
- Pin dependencies with `@latest` in committed code
- Commit `vendor/` unless the course explicitly vendors dependencies
- Skip `go mod tidy` after adding or removing an import
- Ignore `golangci-lint` failures by disabling linters project-wide instead of fixing the code
- Use different module-cache directories per developer without documenting `GOPATH`/`GOMODCACHE`

## Success Criteria

Environment setup is correct when:
- [ ] `go.mod` exists with a real module path and `go 1.23` (or newer) directive
- [ ] `go.sum` is present and untouched by hand edits
- [ ] `.golangci.yml` exists at the project root
- [ ] `.gitignore` excludes binaries, test artifacts, and `.env`
- [ ] `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and `golangci-lint run` all succeed
- [ ] `gofmt -l .` returns no output
